ce", "Riley's large financial advantage: $3.2M on hand vs $0.4M for Oberacker", "Expert consensus of Likely/Safe D across nine raters", "District's recent history of single-digit margins keeps some uncertainty"], "rationale": "Incumbent Josh Riley holds a dominant cash advantage and a favorable expert consensus, while national Republicans abandoning Oberacker removes likely outside spending. The district's history of close finishes and the absence of any general-election polling temper the outlook slightly below Safe D.", "watch": "Any sign of national Republicans re-engaging with outside spending, or Oberacker gaining traction on the utility-cost issue, could narrow the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Josh Riley (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Josh Riley seeks re-election in a Hudson Valley district that has been decided by fewer than three points in each of the last two cycles, though the fundamentals now favor him. He has raised $4.8 million and holds $3.2 million on hand, while Republican Peter Oberacker has raised $1.2 million with under $400,000 available. No outside groups are opposing Riley.\n\nExpert raters uniformly place the race at Likely D or better, and prediction markets price Riley's chances around 95 to 97 percent. The most consequential development is national Republicans withdrawing support from Oberacker in September, effectively ceding the district. Oberacker has also skipped debate invitations, limiting his visibility. Utility costs have emerged as a local campaign theme, but with no independent polling and limited resources, Oberacker faces a steep path.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9635, "margin": 11.98}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9741, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9566, "margin": 11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9622, "margin": 12.0}]}